Z||000000||lowCG||07-13-99||12:02 PM||||What is the front spring wire diameter; coil diameter; and # of coils,(not including the ones on the end that touch anything) on the '88 cars.<br>Never checked these springs out before,since GM made such a wierd choice on the earlier cars,curious now,as so many of you say how much better the '88s are in comparison,and wanted to compare with what I've come up with for requirements on my car.<br>Thanks to someone with calipers!
Z||000001||theogre||07-13-99||02:22 PM||||those dimesnions won't tell you a whole lot. You need to measure the spring rate(s) Physically diferant springs can all have the same rate. Physically identical springs can have differant rates. The type of spring material, the heat treating of it, and a few other things will all change the rate. A spring can have more than one rate as well. such springs get stiffer as you squash them. Spring rate is the number pounds it takes to compress the sping to certain points.<p>A suspension shop may be ably to get you the stock rate data for Fiero. Or tell you who could call. Might even be in some of the GM spec's. Some of the after market makers likely know what the stock rates are, they would need to know that for a starting point on performance parts.
Z||000002||lowCG||07-13-99||04:06 PM||||Thanks,was wondering if anyone had a pair of 6" dial calipers,and an 88' really.<br>Manufacturers specify a spring that falls into a certain useage range,so as not to overstress the spring,or waste materials;from these numbers I can gain information.<br>Better yet,if anyone has a spring tester,and can tear their car apart,measure the rate every half an inch that would be  real nice too.LOL.
